  "query_preview": "(This evening through Monday)...\nIssued at 326 PM EDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nMostly tranquil, albeit warm, weather will continue to be the rule \nacross central Indiana through Wednesday. A signal exists for areas \nof patchy fog to develop across mainly northern portions of central \nIndiana towards daybreak Tuesday. With the ground gradually getting \ncolder and drier, and low temps tonight not expected to be as low as \nlast few nights, don't see the same ground fog development potential \nlike we had the last few nights. Have therefore removed the mention \nof patchy fog in southern areas. Recycled continental air around the \nsurface high pressure moving into the Ohio Valley by Tuesday morning \nand the southern Apps by Wednesday morning will provide for low \ndewpts through Wednesday. Thanks to relatively long nights, this \nwill lead to fairly large diurnal temp durations, characteristic of \nmuch of the western CONUS, but also fairly usual for Indiana in the \nearly Fall. Above normal highs will be mostly in the 80s Tuesday and \nWednesday with upper 80s expected south of I-70 for Wednesday and \nThursday as 850 temps rise to +17 and low level mixing gradually \nincreases owing to an increasing pressure gradient. \n\nThe tendency for rain chances continues to slow down across the \nregion thanks to 2 main factors, 1) the reintensification of the \nupper ridge over the SE CONUS north of a developing Tut low over the \nGulf and 2) the strength of the cyclogenesis over the plains \nassociated with the phasing of the shortwave trough energy over the \nSW CONUS with the remnants of the E. Pacific Hurricane Polo. As a \nresult, despite recent NBM guidance suggesting low pops Thurs \nafternoon in northern regions, there appears to be negligible chances \nof precip until Thursday night across central Indiana. Not until \nFriday does the primary frontal boundary move into central Indiana. \nQPF output varies, but overall 0.5 to 1.5 inches, in line with the \nWPC forecast which seems reasonable. Sufficiently high PWAT, around \n1.5 inches, will support threat for locally heavy rain, but recent \ndry spell and lower river levels should limit hydro concerns. \n\nThe forecast gets a little more complicated by the weekend. The \ntrack of a subtle tropical shortwave out of the Gulf around the \nupper ridge into the SE CONUS will dictate whether another round of \nprecip will move into central Indiana by late Saturday. Model \nconsensus remains that this precip chances should end by Saturday \nafternoon as the primary forcing mechanisms diminish from the west. \nHowever, the AIGFS remains the main outlier showing a secondary \nsurge of tropical moisture with PWATs around 1.7 inches and 30-40 kt \nlow level jet associated with the tropical system aiding in strong \nmass convergence. 24hr QPF amounts from 2.5-3.5 inches are still \nbeing advertised by Sunday morning by this model. \n\nWhether this system take shape or not, the larger scale pattern will \nbecome more dominated by a long wave trough developing over eastern \nCanada and the Northern CONUS. An associated broad area of surface \nhigh pressure will build southward through the Plains and into the \nMS valley by early next week behind an associated cold front. Much \ndrier and seasonably cool weather will follow well into next week.",
